---
// H1–H2: the stubborn node in a toy village (village.ts) that runs a consensus-seeking rule: first a system that
// converges, then one node forced to be stubborn, where a quiet node changes nothing and an important one flips
// everyone (each run waits for its own start); then every node stubborn in turn, for its dynamical importance (the
// thesis says clamped; the slides never do), and what it could be for: a health campaign with three ambassadors and an
// unknown rule of persuasion, the consensus-seeking rule as a stand-in, its three ambassadors against the three
// villagers with the most contacts, and our result. Both slides keep the network in one place, so it stays put from
// one to the next. The limitations, and the score against the network's structure, are for the questions, not the
// slides. Data: src/data/defence/clamp.json.
import Slide from './Slide.astro';
import SimButton from './SimButton.astro';
import clamp from '../../data/defence/clamp.json';
---
<Slide id="ch-impact" label="The stubborn node" builds={6} class="h1" ch={10}>
  <div class="row grow imp-row">
    <div class="imp-view">
      <div class="h1-box">
        <canvas class="net" aria-label="A village of 100 people as a network, running a consensus-seeking rule; then one of them stubborn"></canvas>
        <SimButton class="h1-btn" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col imp-side">
      <div class="imp-head">
        <h2>The stubborn node</h2>
        <p class="imp-sub">Which individuals steer the group?</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="h1-steps">
        <li data-b="1">Find a converging system</li>
        <li data-b="3">Force a node to be stubborn
          <p class="h1-note" data-b="3">Some are not strong enough</p>
          <p class="h1-note" data-b="5">Others convince the entire group</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">The second application uses the consensus-seeking rules that were such bad detectives. Here a toy village of 100 people, about eight contacts each: which individuals steer the group?</p>
  <p slot="say">First, find a converging system: everyone follows a consensus-seeking rule, from a start with half of them on. The click runs it: with nobody stubborn, the village ends with every node on.</p>
  <p slot="say">Then force a node to be stubborn: it stays off, no matter what. Some are not strong enough: when a quiet person at the edge is stubborn (ringed), nobody else follows. Others convince the entire group: when an important person is stubborn, the whole village flips to all off. Holding one node fixed like this is called clamping, or pinning: it is an intervention, not just an observation. (An illustration, from the same start every time.)</p>
</Slide>

<Slide label="Importance score" builds={2} class="h2" ch={10}>
  <div class="row grow imp-row">
    <div class="imp-view">
      <canvas class="net" aria-label="The village with every node coloured by how much of the village follows it when it is stubborn; then three ambassadors ringed, and the three with the most contacts in dashed rings"></canvas>
      <p class="h2-ramp">importance: low <i></i> high</p>
    </div>
    <div class="col imp-side">
      <div class="imp-head">
        <h2>Importance score</h2>
        <p class="imp-sub">Analysing the impact of individuals</p>
      </div>
      <div class="col h2-camp" data-b="1">
        <p class="h2-ex">Example: a health campaign</p>
        <p class="h2-line">Budget: <b>three ambassadors</b></p>
        <p class="h2-line">Real rule unknown: <b>use a stand-in</b></p>
        <p class="h2-key"><i class="amb"></i>our ambassadors<i class="most"></i>most contacts</p>
      </div>
      <p class="result" data-b="2"><b>Our result</b> Rank who steers the group, with a stand-in rule</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">Now make every node stubborn in turn, one at a time, and record how much of the village ends up following it: that is each node’s dynamical importance score, the darker, the more (in the thesis, holding a node fixed is called clamping). In this village most people sway only a small part of it, and the most important about half of it, on average. (Chapter 10, work in progress; an illustration over {clamp.summary.starts} starts that reach agreement, with each node stubborn in each.)</p>
  <p slot="say">Why would we want to know who matters? Take an example: a health campaign with a small budget. Marketing campaigns and political propaganda ask the same question; the health campaign is the friendlier example. In 32 villages in Honduras, researchers started a water-purification and a vitamin programme with a few villagers each, to see how far it would spread, and compared ways of picking those few (Kim and colleagues, The Lancet, 2015). So which three villagers should be our ambassadors? We do not know how villagers really persuade each other, so we use a stand-in: the consensus-seeking rule, not to model the villagers, but to measure them. The three most important, ringed in red, become our ambassadors: an ambassador is a stubborn node, who keeps promoting the programme, no matter what. The dashed rings are the three villagers with the most contacts, 21 each: not the same people. Importance goes with the number of contacts (a rank correlation of {clamp.summary.spearmanEtaDegree.toFixed(2)} here), but not one-to-one: the many contacts of those three also listen to the centre of the village, and follow it. In the Honduran trial too, picking the villagers with the most friends did no better than picking at random, while picking friends that villagers named did better, at least for the vitamins. (An example, not a campaign I studied.)</p>
  <p slot="say">Our result: a ranking of who steers the group that needs no knowledge of the real rule, only a stand-in rule and a simulator. Whether the stand-in picks the same people as the real rule would is the most important next step of Chapter 10: comparing it with, say, an epidemic model on the same networks. Taking the top three one by one also ignores that they may reach the same people; choosing them together is a next step. This part of the thesis is unpublished and still in progress.</p>
</Slide>

<style>
  /* both slides: the network on the left in one and the same place, the title and the words on the right */
  .imp-row { gap: calc(60 * var(--u)); align-items: center; }
  .imp-view { position: relative; width: calc(820 * var(--u)); height: calc(880 * var(--u)); flex: none; }
  .imp-view canvas.net { display: block; width: calc(820 * var(--u)); height: calc(820 * var(--u)); }
  .h1-box { position: relative; }
  .h1-box :global(.h1-btn) { right: calc(10 * var(--u)); bottom: calc(10 * var(--u)); }
  .imp-side { align-self: stretch; padding-top: calc(60 * var(--u)); gap: calc(40 * var(--u)); flex: 1; min-width: 0; }
  .imp-head { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: calc(14 * var(--u)); }
  .imp-sub { font-size: calc(40 * var(--u)); }

  .h1-steps { font-size: calc(50 * var(--u)); gap: calc(30 * var(--u)); padding-left: 1.2em; margin-top: calc(30 * var(--u)); }
  .h1-note { font-size: calc(42 * var(--u)); margin-top: calc(16 * var(--u)); }

  /* the colour of importance, under the network */
  .h2-ramp { position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; top: calc(830 * var(--u)); display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; gap: calc(16 * var(--u)); font-size: calc(36 * var(--u)); color: var(--muted); }
  .h2-ramp i { display: inline-block; width: calc(220 * var(--u)); height: calc(34 * var(--u)); background: linear-gradient(to right, var(--panel), var(--accent)); border: calc(2 * var(--u)) solid var(--line); }
  .h2-camp { gap: calc(24 * var(--u)); margin-top: calc(20 * var(--u)); }
  .h2-ex { font-size: calc(46 * var(--u)); font-weight: 700; }
  .h2-line { font-size: calc(44 * var(--u)); line-height: 1.25; }
  :global(.h2) .result { margin-top: auto; }
  /* the two kinds of ring, as on the network */
  .h2-key { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: calc(16 * var(--u)); font-size: calc(36 * var(--u)); color: var(--muted); margin-top: calc(6 * var(--u)); }
  .h2-key i { width: calc(30 * var(--u)); height: calc(30 * var(--u)); border-radius: 50%; flex: none; }
  .h2-key .amb { border: calc(6 * var(--u)) solid var(--danger); }
  .h2-key .most { border: calc(4 * var(--u)) dashed var(--ink); margin-left: calc(24 * var(--u)); }
</style>

<script>
  import { slide, sim, type How } from '../../scripts/defence/deck';
  import { stage, onSize, ticker } from '../../scripts/defence/stage';
  import { type Rule, density } from '../../scripts/llna';
  import { stepClamped } from '../../scripts/consensus';
  import { build } from '../../scripts/net';
  import { parse, mix, css } from '../../scripts/palette';
  import clamp from '../../data/defence/clamp.json';

  const RULE: Rule = { r: 9, B: 464, S: 488 };
  const n = clamp.deg.length, net = build('ba', n, clamp.edges as [number, number][]);
  const X = (i: number, b: { x: number; w: number }) => b.x + clamp.xy[2 * i] * b.w, Y = (i: number, b: { y: number; h: number }) => b.y + clamp.xy[2 * i + 1] * b.h;
  const radius = (i: number) => 7 + 1.6 * Math.sqrt(clamp.deg[i]);

  /** The network: faint links, then the people, filled when they are on; a ringed person last, over the rest, and a
   * dashed ring in ink round those `dashed` picks. */
  function drawNet(st: NonNullable<ReturnType<typeof stage>>, state: (i: number) => string, ring: (i: number) => string | null, dashed: (i: number) => boolean = () => false) {
    const { ctx, t } = st, b = { x: 30, y: 30, w: st.w - 60, h: st.h - 60 };
    ctx.strokeStyle = t.line; ctx.lineWidth = 1.5; ctx.beginPath();
    for (const [i, j] of clamp.edges) { ctx.moveTo(X(i, b), Y(i, b)); ctx.lineTo(X(j, b), Y(j, b)); }
    ctx.stroke();
    const circle = (i: number, r: number, width: number, color: string) => { ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(X(i, b), Y(i, b), r, 0, 2 * Math.PI); ctx.lineWidth = width; ctx.strokeStyle = color; ctx.stroke(); };
    const person = (i: number) => { ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(X(i, b), Y(i, b), radius(i), 0, 2 * Math.PI); ctx.fillStyle = state(i); ctx.fill(); circle(i, radius(i), 2, t.muted); };
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (!ring(i)) person(i);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { const r = ring(i); if (r) { person(i); circle(i, radius(i) + 10, 13, t.panel); circle(i, radius(i) + 10, 6, r); } }
    ctx.setLineDash([9, 7]);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (dashed(i)) { circle(i, radius(i) + 10, 11, t.panel); circle(i, radius(i) + 10, 4, t.ink); }
    ctx.setLineDash([]);
  }

  // ── H1: the village from the start: a system that converges, nobody stubborn (shown at build 1, run at 2); then a
  // node forced to be stubborn: a quiet one (3, 4), an important one (5, 6) ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.h1')) {
    const c = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.net')!;
    const start = Uint8Array.from(clamp.demo.bits, (ch) => +ch), { hi, lo } = clamp.demo;
    let who = -1, a = start.slice(), b = new Uint8Array(n), tick = 0, cl = new Int8Array(n).fill(-1), at = 0;
    function reset(k: number) {
      who = k; cl.fill(-1); a = start.slice(); tick = 0;
      if (k >= 0) { cl[k] = 0; a[k] = 0; }
    }
    function paint() {
      const st = stage(c); if (!st) return;
      drawNet(st, (i) => (a[i] ? st.t.ink : st.t.panel), (i) => (i === who ? st.t.danger : null));
    }
    const advance = () => {
      stepClamped(a, net, RULE, cl, b); [a, b] = [b, a]; tick++;
      const r = density(a);
      return tick >= 40 || r === 0 || r === 1 || (who >= 0 && r >= 1 - 1.5 / n && tick > 12);
    };
    const run = ticker(s, 3, advance, paint);
    const nodeAt = (k: number) => (k >= 5 ? hi : k >= 3 ? lo : -1);
    sim(s.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]')!, [2, 4, 6], { reset() { run.play(false); reset(nodeAt(at)); paint(); }, play() { run.play(true); } });
    slide(s, {
      // going back to a run shows how it ended; the button's waiting builds reset it
      set(k: number, how: How) {
        at = k;
        if (nodeAt(k) !== who || how !== 'next') { run.play(false); reset(nodeAt(k)); if (how === 'prev' && k % 2 === 0 && k > 0) while (!advance()); }
        paint();
      },
      enter() { reset(-1); },
      leave() { run.play(false); },
    });
    onSize(c, paint);
  }

  // ── H2: everyone's importance on the village, with its colour scale under it (build 0); a health campaign: the three
  // most important ringed in red, as stubborn nodes are, and the three with the most contacts in dashed rings (build 1);
  // our result (2) ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.h2')) {
    const c = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.net')!, eta = clamp.eta, top = Math.max(...eta);
    const three = new Set(eta.map((_, i) => i).sort((a, b) => eta[b] - eta[a]).slice(0, 3));
    const busiest = new Set(clamp.deg.map((_, i) => i).sort((a, b) => clamp.deg[b] - clamp.deg[a] || a - b).slice(0, 3));
    let at = 0;
    const paint = () => {
      const st = stage(c); if (!st) return;
      const lo = parse(st.t.panel), hiC = parse(st.t.accent);
      drawNet(st, (i) => css(mix(lo, hiC, eta[i] / top)), (i) => (at >= 1 && three.has(i) ? st.t.danger : null), (i) => at >= 1 && busiest.has(i));
    };
    slide(s, { set(b: number) { at = b; paint(); } });
    onSize(c, paint);
  }
</script>
